Use this final example to connect **screen code → test code → the configured runner → Bitrise → Sauce Labs results**. MyChickenapp accepts a demo username and password, shows Home on success, and stays on Login with an error on failure.

**Learning fixture.** The values `demo-user` and `demo-only` are synthetic. The app performs local validation with no backend, session token, or real authentication. For an existing product feature, apply these test patterns to its approved authentication contract.

## 1. Understand the test paths

| Test path | Where it runs | What it checks |
|---|---|---|
| Jest policy/component tests | Mac or Bitrise runner. | Validation, state, error rendering, and the Home result. |
| JavaScript Appium device tests | Test code runs in the configured Node runner and controls the installed app on Sauce Labs devices. | Types credentials, taps Sign in, and checks the actual native screen. |
| Native device suites, when selected | Android JUnit instrumentation or iOS XCUITest runs through the configured device integration. | The equivalent screen journey in the native framework. |

The team supports Appium as well as native device suites. **Use the runner actually configured for your project.** The WebdriverIO/Mocha configuration below is a teaching adaptation for Appium, not a verified copy of your private runner setup.

